It appears that NewSystemHandle has been replaced by NewSystemCallback.

It appears that the parameter list of subscribe_on_new_system has changed.

It appears that unsubscribe_on_new_system has been replaced by calling subscribe_on_new_system with null.  It also unsubscribes automatically. 

The get_system function in the examples needs to be updated.  I'm not sure how prom is handled anymore because it used to be passed into the old subscribe_on_new_system.

The new API changes are mentioned here: https://mavsdk.mavlink.io/main/en/cpp/api_reference/classmavsdk_1_1_mavsdk.html

Could I bother someone with a new get_system() function for the examples ?

Here is my attempt.

` // MAVLink Initialization
    Mavsdk mavsdk;      // create a new mavsdk object
    
    // Create a MAVLink connection
    ConnectionResult connection_result = mavsdk.add_any_connection("serial:///dev/ttyUSB0:57600"); 

    // check if we have a connection   
    if (connection_result != ConnectionResult::Success) 
        {
        printf("Connection failed: %s \n" , connection_result);
        return 1;
        }
    else
        printf("MAVLink connected\n"); 

    // get a system from the connection
    auto new_system_promise = std::promise<std::shared_ptr<System>>{};
    auto new_system_future = new_system_promise.get_future();

    mavsdk.subscribe_on_new_system([&mavsdk, &new_system_promise]() {
    new_system_promise.set_value(mavsdk.systems().at(0));
    mavsdk.subscribe_on_new_system(nullptr);
    });

    auto system = new_system_future.get();
    
    //auto system = get_system_id(mavsdk);
    if (!system)
        {
        printf("Could not obtain a system from the MAVLink connection\n");
        return 1;     
        }
    else
        printf("System was obtained from the MAVLink connection\n");`

After some troubleshooting, I found that subscribe_on_new_system never fires.

